import{c as a,Q as r,J as s,h as i,e as t,m as n,l as c,k as l}from"./chunks/framework.WgZQdPFo.js";const w=JSON.parse('{"title":"Finished Goods (Showcase)","description":"Warehouse management of finished products, stock inventory adjustments, and FIFO cost pricing in OmniCraft ERP.","frontmatter":{"title":"Finished Goods (Showcase)","description":"Warehouse management of finished products, stock inventory adjustments, and FIFO cost pricing in OmniCraft ERP.","order":8},"headers":[],"relativePath":"docs/spaces/production/showcase.md","filePath":"docs/spaces/production/showcase.md"}'),d={name:"docs/spaces/production/showcase.md"},h={id:"finished-goods-the-showcase-showroom-inventory",tabindex:"-1"};function u(p,e,m,g,f,y){const o=r("DocIcon");return s(),i("div",null,[t("h1",h,[n(o,{name:"showcase"}),e[0]||(e[0]=c(" Finished Goods: The Showcase & Showroom Inventory ",-1)),e[1]||(e[1]=t("a",{class:"header-anchor",href:"#finished-goods-the-showcase-showroom-inventory","aria-label":'Permalink to "<DocIcon name="showcase" /> Finished Goods: The Showcase & Showroom Inventory"'},"​",-1))]),e[2]||(e[2]=l('<p>The <strong>&quot;Finished Goods&quot;</strong> tab is your warehouse inventory of manufactured products. It displays the active physical balances of all your manufactured items, their actual batch cost price (using MAC moving average cost), and the recommended retail price.</p><p>Products do not appear here out of thin air. For an item to appear in showroom stock, it must pass through the <a href="./workbench">In Production</a> tab (i.e., be completed on the Workbench).</p><hr><h2 id="warehouse-display-modes" tabindex="-1">Warehouse Display Modes <a class="header-anchor" href="#warehouse-display-modes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Warehouse Display Modes&quot;">​</a></h2><p>In the right corner of the top toolbar, you can toggle between two operational layouts:</p><ul><li><strong>Warehouse Rows (Default):</strong> dense operational rows featuring square 1:1 thumbnails, exact physical balances, actual MAC cost price, calculated price, and an instant inventory adjustment button for each item. Ideal for daily high-volume warehouse management.</li><li><strong>Product Cards:</strong> a spacious tile grid of cards displaying full manufacturing counters and extended historical statistics.</li></ul><hr><h2 id="product-card-and-inventory-metrics" tabindex="-1">Product Card and Inventory Metrics <a class="header-anchor" href="#product-card-and-inventory-metrics" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Product Card and Inventory Metrics&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Each item features a square 1:1 thumbnail (loaded from product photos or showcase images) and essential inventory metrics:</p><ul><li><strong>In Stock:</strong> The active physical balance in your showroom.</li><li><strong>Total Manufactured:</strong> A historical counter (how many units of this product you have crafted over your entire history in the program).</li><li><strong>Recommended Retail Price (RRP):</strong> The price automatically computed by the software based on your cost price and global markup (configured in Settings).</li></ul><h3 id="dual-cost-pricing-calculated-vs-actual-stock-cost" tabindex="-1">Dual Cost Pricing (Calculated vs. Actual Stock Cost) <a class="header-anchor" href="#dual-cost-pricing-calculated-vs-actual-stock-cost" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Dual Cost Pricing (Calculated vs. Actual Stock Cost)&quot;">​</a></h3><p>If you have an item in stock, its card displays two distinct cost price values:</p><ol><li><strong>Calculated Cost Price:</strong> The <em>theoretical</em> cost of manufacturing the item right now using your current raw material warehouse stock.</li><li><strong>Actual Stock Cost Price:</strong> The <em>actual</em> cost price of the specific units currently sitting on your shelf. It is retrieved directly from the exact batches you physically completed earlier.</li></ol><div class="info custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Why Use Two Cost Prices? (The FIFO Method)</p><p>Suppose you crafted a bag a month ago when leather was cheaper. The cost price of that bag was <code>$300</code>. The bag was placed on your showroom shelf. Today, leather has significantly increased in price. The Calculator shows that crafting an identical bag right now costs <code>$500</code>. If the program simply updated the cost price of your older bag to <code>$500</code>, your reported profit margins upon sale would be heavily distorted. OmniCraft ERP does not do this. The program remembers that the old <code>$300</code> bag is sitting on the shelf and will deduct exactly that figure when it is sold, ensuring cost-accounting accuracy for your net profit. This is called the <strong>FIFO (First In, First Out)</strong> method.</p></div><hr><h2 id="finished-goods-inventory-adjustments" tabindex="-1">Finished Goods Inventory Adjustments <a class="header-anchor" href="#finished-goods-inventory-adjustments" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Finished Goods Inventory Adjustments&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Just like in the raw materials warehouse, physical reality can sometimes diverge from the software data (e.g., you gifted a wallet to a friend and forgot to log it, or you found a keychain on a shelf that was never completed on the Workbench).</p><p>To correct these discrepancies, every product card features an <strong>&quot;Inventory Adjustment&quot;</strong> button.</p><ol><li>Select the adjustment type: <strong>Record Surplus (+)</strong> or <strong>Record Loss (-)</strong>.</li><li>Specify the quantity.</li><li>Click &quot;Apply&quot;.</li></ol><h3 id="how-inventory-adjustments-affect-your-finances" tabindex="-1">How Inventory Adjustments Affect Your Finances <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-inventory-adjustments-affect-your-finances" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How Inventory Adjustments Affect Your Finances&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Finished goods inventory adjustments <strong>do not impact your Cash Flow ledger</strong>.</p><ul><li>When writing off a loss, the program simply removes the oldest batches (using FIFO) from showroom stock. No money is deducted from your Cash Flow Ledger, as this is purely an inventory correction.</li><li>When recording a surplus, the program adds the item to your showroom shelf and assigns it the <em>current calculated cost price</em> from the Calculator.</li></ul><div class="warning custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Do Not Use Inventory Adjustments for Sales!</p><p>Writing off a loss simply removes the product from stock without generating revenue. If you gave an item to a client, always process it through the <strong>Sales</strong> tab, even if you provided a 100% discount. In that case, simply process the sale with a price of <code>$0</code> so that the program accurately logs the cost price of that item as an operational loss.</p></div><hr><h2 id="warehouse-filtering-and-product-archive-management" tabindex="-1">Warehouse Filtering and Product Archive Management <a class="header-anchor" href="#warehouse-filtering-and-product-archive-management" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Warehouse Filtering and Product Archive Management&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The top toolbar features a three-way status selector:</p><ul><li><strong>&quot;All&quot;:</strong> the full catalog of active workshop products (including items with zero shelf stock).</li><li><strong>&quot;In Stock&quot;:</strong> only products that are physically present on showroom shelves (<code>balance &gt; 0</code>).</li><li><strong>&quot;Archived&quot;:</strong> products discontinued or withdrawn from the active seasonal catalog.</li></ul><h3 id="how-to-move-a-product-to-the-archive" tabindex="-1">How to Move a Product to the Archive <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-to-move-a-product-to-the-archive" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How to Move a Product to the Archive&quot;">​</a></h3><p>If a model has been discontinued or paused for the current manufacturing season:</p><ol><li>Click the <strong>&quot;Archive product&quot;</strong> button (archive icon) on the right side of the item row or card.</li><li>The system verifies active workshop workflows: <ul><li><strong>Work in Progress (WIP) Protection:</strong> If the product is currently being manufactured on the Workbench (<code>in_progress</code>), archiving will be blocked. Complete the order or cancel it first.</li><li><strong>Remaining Stock:</strong> If physical units remain in stock, the software displays a confirmation prompt. You can proceed with archiving: the physical balance and FIFO batches will remain intact, while launching new manufacturing orders on the Workbench will be prohibited.</li></ul></li></ol><div class="tip custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Manufacturing Recipe Integrity</p><p>Archiving a product does not delete its Bill of Materials, labor time, or packaging specs. In the Calculator, the linked recipe is synchronously transferred to the &quot;Archived Recipes&quot; catalog. You can restore it to production at any time.</p></div><h3 id="restoring-and-permanently-deleting-from-archive" tabindex="-1">Restoring and Permanently Deleting from Archive <a class="header-anchor" href="#restoring-and-permanently-deleting-from-archive" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Restoring and Permanently Deleting from Archive&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Switch the toolbar filter to <strong>&quot;Archived&quot;</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Restore Product:</strong> click the unarchive button — the item returns to the active showroom registry, and its recipe becomes active in the Calculator.</li><li><strong>Delete Permanently:</strong> click the delete button (trash can).</li></ul><div class="danger custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Warehouse Capital Protection Guard</p><p>An item can be permanently deleted <strong>only when its stock balance is exactly zero</strong> (<code>0 pcs</code>). If even one unit remains on record, deletion is strictly blocked. First adjust the physical balance to zero via <strong>&quot;Inventory Adjustment&quot;</strong> or process a sale, preventing orphaned FIFO batch costs in your business capital audit.</p></div>',35))])}const b=a(d,[["render",u]]);export{w as __pageData,b as default};
